# Use Jobs from TypeScript

Declare private service queues on the participant:

```trellis
participant "acme.orders-service@v1" service {
  implements "acme.orders@v1";
  job reconcile {
    payload ReconcileInput;
    result ReconcileOutput;
  }
}
```

`ReconcileInput` and `ReconcileOutput` are models in the implemented API. Run
`trellis generate`, then connect with the generated participant as described in
[the service entrypoint guide](/guides/write-a-service/service-entrypoint).

Register the worker before calling `service.wait()`:

```ts
import { Result } from "@qlever-llc/trellis";

service.jobs.reconcile.handle(({ job }) => Result.ok(job.payload));
const job = await service.jobs.reconcile.create(input).orThrow();
const terminal = await job.wait().orThrow();
if (terminal.state === "completed") console.log(terminal.result);
```

This identity worker assumes both models have the same shape; replace its body
with your private work. Return `Result.err(new RetryJobError())` from
`@qlever-llc/trellis/jobs` for retryable failures. Ordinary errors are terminal.
Keep side effects idempotent because a retry can execute the job again.

Jobs are private queues, not caller-facing API surfaces. Use an operation when
another participant needs to start, observe, or cancel a workflow.
